# novadeck kernel source pin — Phase 1
# The TARBALL + its sha256 is the canonical pin. Validated 2026-06-17; re-pinned 2026-07-25
# (7.1.5, HW-validated on Pocket S2 + Pocket ACE); re-pinned 2026-08-03 (7.1.6); re-pinned
# 2026-08-18 (7.2 — mainline, released 2026-08-16; NOT HW-validated yet, see the gate below);
# re-pinned 2026-08-27 (7.2.1); re-pinned 2026-09-03 (7.2.3, issue #86); re-pinned 2026-09-08 (7.2.4);
# re-pinned 2026-09-11 (7.2.5, issue #91 — needs patch 0525, see below).
# 7.2.6 was EVALUATED AND SKIPPED on 2026-09-14 (issue #95, closed) — it breaks Wi-Fi on every
# WCN7850 board. We stay on 7.2.5. Read the 7.2.6 section below BEFORE attempting 7.2.7.
version:    7.2.5
url:        https://cdn.kernel.org/pub/linux/kernel/v7.x/linux-7.2.5.tar.xz
sha256:     55ddf0df8325d9dad96fcff7bd93977d22e3f50af06527572af59b77c7632b78
git_tag:    v7.2.5
git_commit: a300e35c0a4b4a38fb53742ea6e2a203c98ee523

# The sha256 above is the one kernel.org publishes in the SIGNED sha256sums.asc for v7.x, not a
# hash of whatever the CDN happened to serve — re-checked 2026-09-14. The .asc itself verifies as
# a GOOD signature from "Kernel.org checksum autosigner <autosigner@kernel.org>", fingerprint
# B886 8C80 BA62 A1FF FAF5 FDA9 632D 3A06 589D A6B1. That key carries no trust path here, so the
# load-bearing check is CONTINUITY: the SAME signed file still lists 7.2.4 as
# 01710ee01737dac492f1bae52becd057e08d20d11589089aa06accff415c28dd and 7.2.3 as
# 8ba259e8e7b13ec6ef0941c8a39ad90b24bd4a4d6c0010ba6bafb794550ecd03, byte-for-byte the hashes this
# pin carried and shipped before. (The same file lists 7.2.6 as
# 039aef84f2b0994aeda3f4fcfc3d02ec9d7a9bbb9020ea264c43f446c860f606 — verified 2026-09-14 against
# the downloaded tarball, so the skip below is a decision about CONTENT, not about provenance.)
# (Note: keys.openpgp.org serves this key STRIPPED of its user IDs and gpg then refuses it as
# "no public key"; keyserver.ubuntu.com serves it whole.)
# git_commit is the DEREFERENCED tag (`git ls-remote 'v7.2.5^{}'`), matching how the
# 7.1.5/7.1.6/7.2/7.2.1/7.2.3/7.2.4 lines were recorded; the annotated tag object is
# 4c440ba64342e015be9eb06f1ea824eb6e81e562 and is NOT what belongs here.
# (For 7.2.6, had we taken it: tag 500df175a7f9e6bc1a9c328590ca5150f84f9ff0, annotated object
# c148318444bab91e4768a04aad6a18f8d974ecf7.)
#
# 7.2 was MAINLINE with no point release; 7.2.1 was the first stable one. Precedent from
# 7.1.3 -> 7.1.5 used to say a point release INSIDE a series is a pure pin edit. TWO RELEASES
# RUNNING HAVE NOW FALSIFIED THAT, and the second one was not even takeable: 7.2.5 needed a new
# patch (0525) to keep two boards' panels alive, and 7.2.6 broke Wi-Fi on every WCN7850 board and
# was skipped. Treat 'in-series point release' as a prediction about EFFORT, never about RISK.
# Re-run every gate, every time — and note that neither of those two findings came from an
# offline gate. Both came from hardware.
#
# ================= 7.2.6 — EVALUATED AND SKIPPED, 2026-09-14 (issue #95) =================
#
# WE DID NOT TAKE THIS RELEASE. Every offline gate passed and the kernel built clean; the HW gate
# then found TWO regressions no offline gate could see. One we fixed. The other kills Wi-Fi on
# every WCN7850 board and had no identifiable cause after eliminating five candidates, so the pin
# went back to 7.2.5 — which is HW-validated on four boards — and #95 was closed. That is an
# outcome the issue explicitly allows: "a point release we decide to skip stays skipped, and the
# title dedupe will not re-file it."
#
# THIS SECTION IS KEPT FOR THE NEXT BUMP, NOT AS HISTORY. Almost everything below is still true of
# 7.2.7 and later, because the changes that caused it are IN the stable series now:
#   - the bonded-DSI revert is still upstream, so 0534 WILL BE NEEDED AGAIN. The patch body is
#     regenerable in two commands (see REGRESSION 1) and was HW-CONFIRMED to work.
#   - the ath12k/WCN7850 Wi-Fi break is unexplained and therefore probably still there. TEST WI-FI
#     ON A WCN7850 BOARD EARLY on the next bump, before investing in anything else.
#   - 0511 and 0512 land upstream at 7.2.6, so they become deletions again the moment we move.
#   - 0508's deletion is the one change from this attempt we KEPT: it was wrong on its own merits,
#     independent of which kernel we pin. See below and issue #96 (closed).
#
# RUN THE INTERSECTION FIRST — and this release is why that is not a formality. 7.2.5 -> 7.2.6
# changes 1764 files (7.2.4 -> 7.2.5 changed 462); our patches touch 173; the INTERSECTION IS
# NINETEEN FILES, against two at the previous bump. All nineteen were read. Recipe unchanged:
# take the `+++ b/` paths out of https://cdn.kernel.org/pub/linux/kernel/v7.x/incr/patch-7.2.5-6.xz
# and intersect with the `+++ b/` paths in kernel/patches/*.patch.
#
# WHAT A MOVE TO 7.2.6-OR-LATER OWES THE PATCH SET — three deletions and one addition. Only the
# 0508 deletion was applied (it stands on its own merits); the rest are queued for whenever we do
# move. The addition (0534) is not an offline finding — it came out of the HW gate, REGRESSION 1:
#
#   0511 -> DELETE ON THE NEXT MOVE (restored here, still load-bearing on 7.2.5).
#     "drm/msm: remove objects from evit list after pinning them" (Anna Maniscalco)
#     landed in 7.2.6 verbatim, upstream's backport carrying the same "evit" typo in its subject.
#     `patch` reported it as "Reversed (or previously applied)", and the pristine 7.2.6 tree has
#     exactly one drm_gpuvm_bo_evict(vm_bo, false) in msm_gem_vm_bo_validate(). Pure deletion.
#
#   0512 -> DELETE ON THE NEXT MOVE (restored here, still load-bearing on 7.2.5).
#     7.2.6 backports all three of Konrad Dybcio's "Remove brownout risks from DPU"
#     patches (54b17a858b8b dpu, e532b800728d dp, 00008e6f544c dsi), which is exactly what our
#     0512 squashed. Our DPU early return, our DSI removal and three of our four DP removals are
#     now upstream's own.
#     THE FOURTH DP SITE STAYS IN THE TREE, and this is the part worth reading, because the first
#     attempt at this bump kept a reduced 0512 to remove it and that was WRONG.
#     7.2.x dp_ctrl.c has four dev_pm_opp_set_rate(ctrl->dev, 0) sites, mainline had three. The
#     surviving one is in msm_dp_ctrl_off_link_stream(). The tempting inference — "same code,
#     mainline just renamed it, so remove ours too" — does not survive reading the bodies:
#       7.2.x msm_dp_ctrl_off       vsc_sdp mainlink_disable ctrl_reset pixclk link_clk_dis phy_off
#       mainline msm_dp_ctrl_off_link  vsc_sdp mainlink_disable ctrl_reset    link_clk_dis phy_off
#       7.2.x off_link_stream  vsc_sdp PSM_CONFIG mainlink_disable pixclk link_clk_dis phy_off
#                              PHY_EXIT PHY_INIT   (and no ctrl_reset)
#     Konrad's third hunk is in msm_dp_ctrl_off_link, whose 7.2.x counterpart is msm_dp_ctrl_off —
#     and the backport de-voted that one, correctly. off_link_stream is a DIFFERENT function
#     (dongle-to-D3 psm_config, phy reinit), and mainline did not rename it, it DELETED the path:
#     the sink_count==0 branch in dp_display.c is now just `msm_dp_display_host_phy_exit(dp)`,
#     with no ctrl teardown and no vote at all. So there is NO upstream line corresponding to
#     removing this one, and no upstream author wrote, reviewed or tested such a removal.
#     We therefore leave it. 7.2.6 ships dp_ctrl.c exactly as stable intends, one vote site.
#     If that pattern is genuinely harmful on the dongle-unplug path, it is an upstream
#     conversation, not a local patch.
#     TWO TRAPS PAID FOR HERE, both worth keeping:
#       - GNU patch skips the WHOLE FILE at the first reversed hunk and then reports "4 out of 4
#         hunks ignored", which reads as "all four are upstream" and is false. Count the call
#         sites in the pristine tree; never believe the hunk arithmetic.
#       - "mainline does not have this line" is NOT evidence that mainline removed it. Check
#         whether the function still exists upstream at all, and if it does not, find where its
#         CALLERS went. A function deleted in a refactor will never be "fixed" upstream, so a
#         DROP-check written against it can never trip.
#
#   0508 DELETED — APPLIED, and this one is permanent regardless of the pin. NOT because upstream
#     took it. It was the SUPERSEDED VERSION of an unmerged
#     fix, which is a state worth learning to recognise, because every cheap check says "keep it":
#     7.2.6 still has plain kfree() in msm_ringbuffer_destroy() and msm_gem_vm_free(), mainline at
#     7.3-rc3 still has both, drm_sched_fini() still does not detach fences from the scheduler,
#     drm_sched_fence_get_timeline_name() still returns fence->sched->name, and 7.2.6 does not
#     touch drivers/gpu/drm/scheduler/ at all. The bug is real and still open. The patch was still
#     the wrong thing to carry.
#     WHAT THE LIST SAYS. The header credited Dmitry Baryshkov; the actual author is Jonghyuk Kim
#     (MalHyuk) <malhyuk97@gmail.com>, msgid 20260902012720.880783-1-malhyuk97@gmail.com. Their
#     posting history (patchwork.kernel.org /api/1.2/patches/?submitter=224493) reads:
#       2026-08-28  [v1,1/2] drm/sched: cache the timeline name to fix a use-after-free
#       2026-09-02  [v2..] and [v3..] of that same core series
#       2026-09-02  drm/msm | drm/nouveau | accel/amdxdna: RCU-free the scheduler-containing ...
#       2026-09-04  [v4,1/3] drm/sched: cache the timeline name to fix a use-after-free
#     The three per-driver RCU patches were a one-day detour, and v4 says so outright: "amdxdna,
#     nouveau and msm (VM_BIND) are still affected in mainline, so fix it in the CORE to cover any
#     per-context-scheduler driver at once." v4 caches sched->name in the fence at init so
#     get_timeline_name() never dereferences fence->sched, which makes RCU-freeing the container
#     unnecessary. Same bug class as CVE-2025-38703 (xe) and CVE-2025-71302 (panthor), both fixed
#     per-driver — and not repeating that is the point of v4.
#     SO WE CARRIED v1 OF AN APPROACH ITS OWN AUTHOR REPLACED TWO DAYS LATER. Not taking v4
#     instead: it is core drm/sched, it affects every driver on the box, patchwork still shows it
#     `new` with no trailer but the author's own sign-off, and it imposes a contract (the @name
#     passed to drm_sched_init() must outlive any exported fence) that we would then own
#     verifying. Recorded in issue #96 (opened then CLOSED as not-tracking: the bug is upstream's
#     to fix and we have never observed it here). Do not resurrect 0508 — the rule that catches
#     this class is in the gate block below, under "check the AUTHOR, not the thread".
#     THE CHECK THAT WOULD HAVE CAUGHT IT, and it is now part of the drill below: for any patch we
#     carry from a list, look up the AUTHOR's other postings, not just the patch's own thread.
#     A superseded patch's thread looks exactly like a patch awaiting review — one message, no
#     replies, state `new`. The supersession lives in a DIFFERENT thread.
#
# WHAT THE OTHER SIXTEEN INTERSECTING FILES TURNED OUT TO BE — read, and benign for us:
#   drivers/gpu/drm/msm/disp/dpu1/dpu_encoder.c    upstream swaps msm_dp_is_yuv_420_enabled() for
#     drm_mode_is_420_only() in update_topology; our 0510/0515 hunks are elsewhere in the file.
#   drivers/gpu/drm/msm/msm_drv.h                  the declarations that swap drops. 0515 only.
#   drivers/gpu/drm/msm/disp/dpu1/dpu_hw_catalog.c adds _DMA_SBLK_V13() + dpu_dma_sblk_v13 for
#     DPU v13 (sm8750). Our 0528/0530 do not touch that region; no v13 board here.
#   drivers/gpu/drm/msm/msm_gpu.c                  put_task_struct() leak fix + recover-before-
#     retire reorder, both in recover_worker(); our 0523 hunk is in
#     msm_gpu_create_private_vm(), ~370 lines away.
#   drivers/gpu/drm/msm/msm_ringbuffer.c/.h        the sched_initialized fix (e2332abed2a4). It
#     used to collide with 0508's context; with 0508 gone we simply inherit it.
#   drivers/gpu/drm/msm/msm_gem_vma.c              the 0511 landing — see above.
#   drivers/gpu/drm/msm/adreno/a6xx_catalog.c      RBBM_CLOCK_CNTL3_TP0 fix in a730_hwcg[].
#     a730_hwcg has exactly ONE consumer, chip_id 0x07030001 (sm8450). Our three SoCs take
#     a650_hwcg (0x06050002, sm8250) and a740_hwcg (0x43050a01 sm8550 / 0x43051401 sm8650).
#     Our 0004 (IFPC on a740) is untouched.
#   drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath12k/wmi.c          19 upstream hunks, none ours. The nearest,
#     "Correctly copy the hint BSSID in WMI scan request", is in the same function our 0507
#     patches (ath12k_wmi_send_scan_start_cmd) but ~100 lines past it; 0507 applied at fuzz 0.
#     0507 is still NOT upstream — cmd->scan_priority is still unassigned in 7.2.6.
#   drivers/video/backlight/Kconfig                BACKLIGHT_KTD253 drops `|| COMPILE_TEST`.
#     Our 0060/0062 add their own entries further down.
#   kernel/sys.c                                   prctl_set_auxv() memcpy length fix; our 0504 is
#     in the compat-input-syscall path.
#   arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm8{250,450,550,650,750}.dtsi  PCIe iommu-map entries gain the
#     missing 4th cell (<0x0 &apps_smmu 0x1400 0x1> -> <0x0 &apps_smmu 0x1400 0x0 0x1>). No board
#     DTS of ours overrides iommu-map, so we simply inherit the fix. sm8750 also gains qcom,ice on
#     ufs; not a SoC we build for.
#
# THE ONE THING THE INTERSECTION FOUND THAT ONLY HARDWARE CAN SETTLE: 7.2.6 REWORKS THE SM8250
# IRIS/VENUS NODE, in sm8250.dtsi, which all three of our SM8250 boards inherit wholesale — each
# carries nothing but `&venus { status = "okay"; };` (Pocket Max, AYN Thor Lite, Retroid Pocket
# common). Two upstream commits:
#   arm64: dts: qcom: sm8250: correct frequencies in the Iris OPP table
#     720/1014/1098/1332 MHz -> 240/338/366/444 MHz. The old table was the vcodec clock's rates
#     written against the core clock; every OPP the driver picks therefore changes.
#   arm64: dts: qcom: sm8250: sort out Iris power domains
#     adds RPMHPD_MMCX as a fourth power domain ("mmcx") and gives every OPP a SECOND
#     required-opps entry, so the MX and MMCX votes are now driven independently.
# Nothing offline can grade this. It is the reason the HW gate below must include a real decode on
# an SM8250 board: `ffmpeg -c:v h264_v4l2m2m` and NOT Steam — iris loads its firmware lazily at
# STREAMON and logs nothing on success, so /dev/video0 existing proves nothing. See
# kernel-build.sh's venus/iris comments and build a clip on-device with
# `ffmpeg -f lavfi -i testsrc=size=1280x720:rate=30:duration=2 ...`; ffmpeg IS on the dev image.
#
# OFFLINE GATES, all re-run on 2026-09-14 against a pristine 7.2.6 extraction:
#   - all 90 patches apply CUMULATIVELY in lexical order with ZERO rejects (93 before this bump;
#     0508, 0511 and 0512 all deleted).
#   - the PER-HUNK fuzz profile is BYTE-IDENTICAL to 7.2.5's: 18 hunks at fuzz 1 and 12 at fuzz 2,
#     same patches, same hunk numbers, on both sides. Nothing climbed. (The raw total moved 26 ->
#     30 between the 7.2.5 gate and now for OUR reasons — 0526-0530 were added on main in the
#     meantime — which is exactly why the total is the wrong instrument and the profile is the
#     right one. Both sides were run today, against fresh pristine extractions: the 7.2.5 side
#     with the PRE-bump patch set, all 93 applying with zero rejects, and the 7.2.6 side with the
#     surviving 90. That asymmetry costs the comparison nothing — none of the three deleted
#     patches contributes a fuzz line on either side.)
#   - "0 rejects" does not prove anything about what a DELETION leaves behind, so the three
#     removals were asserted in the patched tree: exactly ONE
#     dev_pm_opp_set_rate(ctrl->dev, 0) left in dp_ctrl.c, exactly one
#     drm_gpuvm_bo_evict(vm_bo, false) (0511's code, now upstream's), and ZERO kfree_rcu in
#     msm_ringbuffer.c and msm_gem_vma.c. I.e. all three files now read exactly as stable ships
#     them, which is the whole point of the three deletions.
#   - 0525 is STILL LOAD-BEARING and unchanged. Its own drop check says to look for the absence of
#     clk_round_rate() in dsi_calc_clk_rate_6g() and for byte_intf_clk_div_2 in struct
#     msm_dsi_host: 7.2.6's dsi_host.c differs from 7.2.5's by the brownout hunk ALONE, so neither
#     is true and the patch stays. Beware a false positive here — pristine dsi_host.c already
#     contains `phy_shared_timings->byte_intf_clk_div_2`, a DIFFERENT, pre-existing field. Grep
#     for the struct member, not the bare name.
#   - 0526's drop check is also still unmet: qcom_battmgr.c is not in 7.2.6's delta at all, and
#     `battmgr->unit = QCOM_BATTMGR_UNIT_mAh` is still absent from qcom_battmgr_probe().
#   - all 426 referenced CONFIG_* symbols (kernel.config + trim-platforms.config) exist in the
#     PATCHED 7.2.6 tree, NONE missing. Count unchanged from 7.2.5 (426) and neither fragment was
#     edited.
#   - nothing else of OURS landed upstream this release: every remaining patch applied FORWARD, so
#     0511 and 0512 are the only deletion candidates 7.2.6 creates.
#   - and the whole thing was then BUILT: `make kernel` against 7.2.6 on 2026-09-14, exit 0, zero
#     compiler errors, all 90 patches applied, `trim-platforms: 46 symbols confirmed disabled`
#     with no redundant list and no requested symbol landing on the wrong value. Image + all 21
#     board DTBs + 947 modules under /lib/modules/7.2.6. That is the gate the offline drill cannot
#     give you.
#     47 compiler warnings — the SAME COUNT 7.2.5 produced, and this time the file census was
#     kept: all 47 are in OUR out-of-tree drivers (34 chipone_tddi, 4 qcom-hv-haptics, 1 each
#     ayaneo-serial-mcu / qcom-spmi-haptics / rmi_driver, 1 include/linux/device.h expansion), i.e.
#     in files 7.2.6 does not touch, so none of them can be bump fallout. Module count moved
#     946 -> 947; nothing in kernel.config changed, so that is an olddefconfig-resolved upstream
#     default and not a decision of ours.
#
# HW GATE FOR 7.2.6 — RUN ON THREE BOARDS, AND IT IS WHAT KILLED THE BUMP. The roster and what each board
# proves are unchanged; they are in STANDING FACTS immediately below, along with the
# compatible-string reading that says which boards can exercise 0525 at all.
#
#   Pocket Max  sm8250  PASS 2026-09-14 (build 98f72ff / 20260914T155747Z). The iris gate — the
#     mandatory new one this bump — is GREEN: the DT rework is live on the device
#     (power-domain-names "venus vcodec0 mx mmcx", opp-table 240/338/366/444, pm_genpd `mmcx on`),
#     and a real `ffmpeg -c:v h264_v4l2m2m` decode returned frame=60 with zero errors and an
#     output of EXACTLY 82,944,000 bytes = 1280*720*1.5*60. That byte count is the load-bearing
#     evidence: dmesg is SILENT on success because iris loads firmware lazily at STREAMON, so a
#     clean log proves nothing on its own. Panel up, gamescope on DSI-1, Steam up, 0 failed units.
#     Display-wise this board is only a NEGATIVE CONTROL (`qcom,sm8250-dsi-ctrl`, plain 6g ops).
#
#   Pocket ACE  sm8550  FAIL — Wi-Fi.    Pocket S2  sm8650  FAIL — Wi-Fi AND display.
#
# REGRESSION 1 — DUAL-DSI, half the panel. DIAGNOSED AND FIXED, HW-CONFIRMED, patch since
#   removed with the pin. RE-CREATE IT on the next move; it is two commands, below. 7.2.6 backports 5de981b7db1f
#   (upstream 44784327815b), a REVERT of 93c97bc8d85d "drm/msm: dsi: fix PLL init in bonded mode".
#   Pocket S2 is the only bonded board we ship (`qcom,dual-dsi-mode` + `qcom,sync-dual-dsi`, one
#   1440x2560 panel over two links) and only its BOTTOM HALF draws on 7.2.6. Two PHYs share one
#   PLL in bonded mode; the restored pre-fix code early-returns out of dsi_pll_enable_pll_bias()
#   for the second PHY and force-assigns pll_enable_cnt = 1 in dsi_7nm_phy_enable(), so the second
#   link never gets its bias. 0534 re-applies 93c97bc8d85d. Upstream reverted it because it broke
#   the NON-bonded case and they are "letting Neil to work on a better fix" — so upstream has no
#   state that is right for a fleet with both kinds of panel, and we pick the one we MEASURED:
#   7.2.5 carried 93c97bc8d85d and all four gated boards passed, the three non-bonded ones
#   included. HW-CONFIRMED on Pocket S2 2026-09-14: with the re-revert in, the whole 1440x2560
#   panel draws again.
#   TO RE-CREATE THE PATCH (never hand-author a .patch body):
#     for f in drivers/gpu/drm/msm/dsi/phy/dsi_phy.h drivers/gpu/drm/msm/dsi/phy/dsi_phy_7nm.c; do
#       tar -xOf linux-<NEW>.tar.xz  linux-<NEW>/$f  > old/$f   # the reverted (broken) content
#       tar -xOf linux-7.2.5.tar.xz  linux-7.2.5/$f > new/$f    # the content we want back
#       diff -u old/$f new/$f   # -> the hunks, with a/ b/ headers fixed up
#     done
#   Assert afterwards, because zero rejects proves nothing about a revert: `pll_data` absent from
#   both files, exactly one `pll->pll_enable_cnt++;`, and no `pll_enable_cnt = 1;`.
#   DROP IT INSTEAD if Neil Armstrong's rework has landed — the tell is that
#   dsi_pll_enable_pll_bias() no longer early-returns on a non-zero pll_enable_cnt.
#
# REGRESSION 2 — ath12k / WCN7850 Wi-Fi DEAD. UNRESOLVED, AND IT IS WHY WE SKIPPED 7.2.6.
#   `failed to set mhi state: POWER_ON(2)` / `failed to start mhi: -110`, deterministic, on BOTH
#   WCN7850 boards; no wlan0 at all. Pocket Max is unaffected because it is `pci17cb,1101` =
#   ath11k, a different driver — which is exactly why Max passing is NOT evidence that Wi-Fi is
#   healthy on this pin. Read the wifi node's compatible, not the board name.
#   ELIMINATED BY INSPECTION, so do not re-walk these: the two bus/mhi/host changes (an EDL-only
#   error path, and a read-back inside mhi_soc_reset(), which ath12k does not call on power-up —
#   it uses its own ath12k_pci_sw_reset()); the QMP PCIe PHY `.pcs_lane1` addition (offsets_v5 is
#   sm8550-only and sm8650 uses offsets_v6_20, yet BOTH fail, so the cause must be SoC-independent);
#   pcie-qcom.c's new PERST check (it only skips when the GPIO provider is a CHILD of the PCIe
#   node, and &tlmm is not); and the 19 ath12k commits themselves — ath12k/pci.c and ath12k/mhi.c,
#   which own MHI power-up, are NOT in the delta at all, so reverting the ath12k files that DID
#   change cannot move a POWER_ON timeout.
#   ALSO ELIMINATED, BY EXPERIMENT ON HARDWARE: drivers/pci/pcie/aspm.c. It was the best remaining
#   candidate — the only GENERIC-PCI file in the delta on this path, and ath12k_pci_power_up()
#   carries "Disable ASPM during firmware download due to problems switching to AMSS state", AMSS
#   being exactly the transition that times out (CONFIG_PCIEASPM=y, so the changed code is live).
#   A card built with that ONE file reverted to 7.2.5 was booted on Pocket S2 2026-09-14: the
#   panel came back (that was 0534) and Wi-Fi did NOT. So aspm.c is out too.
#   THE BEST UNTESTED HYPOTHESIS, and the experiment that settles it — start here next time.
#   The symptom is sharper than "PCIe is broken": on BOTH boards both endpoints enumerate, link
#   up and bind, and MMIO/config/BAR access all work. What fails is COMPLETION —
#   `xhci-pci-renesas: Error while assigning device slot ID: Command Aborted` and MHI timing out
#   fetching firmware. The UART-attached gamepad MCU is unaffected. That is a device-side DMA (or
#   MSI-completion) failure with the host side healthy.
#   And 7.2.6 flips exactly that: apps_smmu declares `#iommu-cells = <2>`, of_map_id() requires
#   `3 + cells` = FIVE cells per iommu-map entry, and 7.2.5 supplies FOUR. So ON THE PIN WE SHIP
#   the PCIe iommu-map is MALFORMED, the parse fails, and these devices run with NO IOMMU mapping
#   at all. 7.2.6's fleet-wide "Fix the PCIe iommu-map entries" (22 boards) makes it parse — i.e.
#   flips our PCIe devices from bypass to TRANSLATED — and that is when DMA stops completing.
#   AGAINST IT: 22 platforms got the same change, so a universal break would be a huge upstream
#   regression. FOR IT: we define no iommu-map of our own, our PCIe DTS is not upstream-shaped,
#   and nothing else in the delta explains both devices on both SoCs.
#   THE EXPERIMENT, one build, one boot, single variable: apply ONLY the 4->5 cell rewrite to the
#   pinned dtsi (sm8250 6 entries, sm8550 4, sm8650 4 — counts matching upstream; do NOT touch
#   msi-map, whose #msi-cells is 1 so its 4-cell form is already right). Wi-Fi and gamepad dying
#   identifies the cause; surviving exonerates it. Deliberately NOT run on 7.2.5 today: it would
#   break a known-good pin to buy an answer we cannot act on while we are not moving. Run it on
#   the NEXT bump, where the kernel is being built anyway and the answer is actionable.
#   NOTE INDEPENDENT OF ALL THIS: running PCIe devices with no IOMMU mapping means the Wi-Fi chip
#   and the USB3 controller have had NO DMA isolation on every pin we have shipped. That is worth
#   fixing on its own merits, not only as a bump blocker.
#
#   NOTHING ELSE IS LEFT that inspection can reach. The next step, if anyone wants 7.2.6 specifically,
#   is an area bisect — revert drivers/pci/ + drivers/phy/ + drivers/bus/mhi/ together, then
#   split — at 2-4 build-and-boot cycles. We judged that not worth it against simply waiting for
#   7.2.7, which is the decision recorded at the top of this section.
#
# STILL OWED WHENEVER WE DO MOVE: 0525 on the two v2.9 boards (S2, FIT), and the SMMU-v3 (0515)
# gate, which needs Pocket FIT specifically — it is the only DSC panel. Neither was reachable on
# 7.2.6: S2 could not be judged on 0525 while half its panel was dark, and FIT was never booted.
#
# WHERE THE LOGS ARE, because this cost time: /var/log is BIND-MOUNTED onto /home by our own
# var-log.mount, so the journal is NOT under novadeck-var-*/log/journal (that dir holds only an
# empty `remote/`). Read a card with
#   journalctl -D /run/media/<you>/novadeck-home/.novadeck/offload/var/log/journal --list-boots
# and note that `-b -1` does NOT work with -D — it reports "no persistent journal was found".
# Pass the explicit boot ID from --list-boots instead. (Second instance of the -b trap in
# [[journalctl-traps]].)
#
# AND A KNOCK-ON WORTH KNOWING: the "stuck at novadeck first-boot grow" seen on ACE was NOT a grow
# bug. grow-home.sh does a bare `udevadm settle` after systemd-repart, and settle waits on the
# WHOLE udev queue — including the 90-second ath12k probe. It returned 0.2s after ath12k gave up.
# The grow itself was correct ("No changes", home already filling the card). Its one error,
# `resize2fs: Please run e2fsck -f first`, is the expected consequence of pulling the card out of
# a running board, and is non-fatal by design.
#
# ===================== STANDING FACTS ABOUT THE HW GATE (any pin) =====================
#
# Per-release PASS narratives are NOT kept here — a gate that passed on a pin we no longer ship
# tells the next reader nothing. Git history has them. What survives is only what still decides
# how to run the next gate.
#
# THE ROSTER, and what each board can and cannot prove:
#   Pocket ACE  qcs8550-ayaneo-pocketace  panel 0051 ayaneo,pocket-ace-panel   VIDEO mode
#   Pocket S2   sm8650-ayaneo-ps2         panel 0066 ayaneo,wt0630-2k          VIDEO mode
#   Pocket FIT  sm8650-konkr-pf           panel 0067 ayaneo,ar14-144hz         VIDEO mode
#   Pocket Max  sm8250-mangmi-pocket-max  panel 0058 mangmi,pocket-max-panel   COMMAND mode
# Four of 21 boards and three SoCs; the other 17 are untested on every pin so far. Both DSI modes
# on purpose — the deferred-scanout-release premise in 0515 is video-mode-specific, so the
# command-mode board is there to show the other path did not regress.
#
# READ THE DSI HOST'S `compatible` ON THE DEVICE, NOT THE BOARD NAME, to know which board proves
# what about 0525: `qcom,sm8650-dsi-ctrl` binds msm_dsi_6g_v2_9_host_ops (the runtime-reparenting
# ops, so 0525 is live) — Pocket S2 and Pocket FIT. `qcom,sm8550-dsi-ctrl` and the sm8250 one bind
# the plain 6g ops, so 0525 cannot fire in either direction: ACE and Max are NEGATIVE CONTROLS,
# proving only that nothing else broke display bring-up. The regression's signature to grep for is
# "Failed to set rate pixel clk", "rcg didn't update", "Turning OFF PHY while PLL is on".
#
# A DCS BRIGHTNESS ROUND-TRIP DOES NOT TEST 0525. With our 0005, xfer_prepare() skips the
# link-clock cycle whenever the display is active (`xfer_owns_link_clks = !(power_on && enabled)`),
# so a write to a live panel never reaches dsi_link_clk_set_rate_6g() and never reaches the
# rounding. For us that rounding runs at modeset/power_on only — i.e. ANY boot of a v2.9 board
# tests it and nothing else does. Corollary: do not drop 0005 imagining 0525 covers for it, and do
# not expect 0525 to produce a visible improvement.
#
# A NEW Image INVALIDATES THE WHOLE `/lib/modules/<ver>` TREE (module BTF). Testing a pin means
# flashing a card built from it, never swapping /boot/Image on an existing card.
#
# KNOWN BOOT NOISE, do not re-diagnose it as fallout from whatever you just changed:
#   RESUME, all boards — `IRQ168: set affinity failed(-22)`. Known on these SoCs, appears on a
#     suspend cycle that otherwise completes correctly. Not worth investigating.
#   synaptics_dsx_i2c retry storm ("I2C retry 1..10", "I2C read over retry limit",
#     "Failed to read interrupt status") — KNOWN ON THESE SoCs and NOT related to suspend/resume,
#     despite showing up inside a suspend window in any log that spans one. Do not read it as
#     suspend fallout; it is ongoing touchscreen-driver noise that happens to land there.
#   Pocket ACE — a bounded burst of 10 arm-smmu context faults at ~0.77s, all cbfrsynra=0x1c00
#     (the MDSS node, `iommus = <&apps_smmu 0x1c00 0x2>`), inside a 0.4 ms window, all of them
#     BEFORE the panel rails come up at 0.854s. The bootloader continuous-splash handoff, i.e.
#     what dpu-quiesce-boot-scanout.patch is for. Board-specific: Pocket FIT shows ZERO on the
#     same kernel. STILL OPEN: whether it predates our own 0514/0518-0524. Nobody has checked.
#
# SMMU SERIES AT v3 since 2026-09-12, and STILL NOT HW-GATED — it is the oldest ungated thing on
# this pin. 0515/0516/0517 used to carry patches 1-3 of Dmitry Baryshkov's v2 "drm/msm: fix SMMU
# fault dumps"; they are now ONE file, 0515-drm-msm-fix-smmu-fault-dumps.patch, carrying v3
# patches 1, 2, 4, 5, 6 and 7. The offline side is clean and has been re-run on every tree since,
# 7.2.6 included: applies at zero fuzz, the v3 code asserted PRESENT in the patched tree
# (msm_fb_unpin_work, msm_framebuffer_unpin, kzalloc_obj(*unpin), the DSC ternary, mutex_init
# before drm_framebuffer_init) and the v2 code asserted GONE (msm_kms_init_fb_unpin, the per-crtc
# drm_flip_work). kzalloc_obj(), which v3's 4/8 uses, is in include/linux/slab.h; no shim.
# WHAT IS OWED: hardware. v3's 4/8 is a REWRITE of the old 0517 on the display teardown path, so
# the four-board gate has to clear it — POCKET FIT MOST OF ALL, since it is the DSC board (0067 is
# DSC-only, slice_count 2) and therefore the only one that exercises v3's 6/8.
# The fallback is narrower than a pin revert: `git checkout` the three v2 files and drop 0515.
#
# 7/8 ADDED 2026-09-13, reversing the previous day's skip. The skip reasoning was that
# crtc_state->ctm is never non-NULL for us because gamescope does colour in its own Vulkan
# composite. DPU inline rotation (0528/0529) put gamescope on DIRECT SCANOUT, where that composite
# never runs — CONFIRMED on Pocket FIT 2026-09-13: night mode at maximum is invisible, and appears
# the instant GAMESCOPE_COMPOSITE_FORCE=1 is set. The fix keeps scanout and drives the DPU's DSPP
# CTM + GAMMA_LUT instead, which makes 7/8 load-bearing (its own upstream reproducer is a CTM
# test) and promotes 5/8 from NULL-safety to a live fix. Full rationale in 0515's own preamble.
# The drill above was RE-RUN with 7/8 in: still 90 patches, ZERO rejects, and the per-hunk fuzz
# profile is unchanged at 16 hunks at fuzz 1 and 10 at fuzz 2, with nothing inside 0515 needing
# fuzz. 7/8 also touches msm_atomic.c, which no other patch we carry does.
# CONSEQUENCE FOR USERSPACE, and it is not optional: 7/8 removes the kernel-side
# `state->allow_modeset = true` override, so ENABLING colour management now requires the client to
# commit with DRM_MODE_ATOMIC_ALLOW_MODESET itself. Anything that sets a CTM on a plain atomic
# flip gets -EINVAL instead of the silent modeset the hack used to grant it.

# LUTDMA ADDED 2026-09-13 — 0530/0531/0532. Third-party (the author's tree, against 7.2.3),
# carried here re-rolled onto the pinned tree and re-drilled on every bump since. What it is:
# on DPU 9.0 and later the colour LUT blocks sit behind a LUT bus with NO memory-mapped write path,
# reachable only by the downstream sde LUTDMA engine — which is why mainline exposes only PCC and
# GC on these SoCs, and why our CRTC shows CTM + GAMMA_LUT and no DEGAMMA_LUT. That is a driver
# limit, not a hardware one. 0530 ports the engine (v2 for DPU 9.x, v3 for 10.x/12.x) and uses it
# to drive the DSPP inverse gamma, exposed as a 256-entry CRTC DEGAMMA_LUT, and the 17^3 gamut
# block, exposed as a DPU_3D_LUT blob; 0531 adds the optional "lutdma" reg entry to the binding and
# 0532 fills it in for sm8550 (0xaeac000/0x800) and sm8650/sm8750 (0xaf80000/0x7000). Without the
# reg entry the properties are simply not exposed, so the patch is inert on anything that lacks it.
# That lifts the constraint gamescope's 0014 was built around — only a DIAGONAL is expressible with
# no degamma — and lets the whole SDR chain scan out in hardware (gamescope 0018).
#
# THE RE-ROLL, precisely: all three apply CUMULATIVELY with ZERO rejects and — worth noting
# against the fuzz-climb warning below — ZERO fuzz and ZERO offsets.
# Two hunks of the author's dpu_crtc.c did not apply and were DROPPED rather than
# duplicated, because 0515 already carries the same two upstream changes: the ternary form of the
# mixer's hw_dspp assignment, and dropping color_mgmt_changed from the condition that reassigns
# resources. What remains at that site is only what is new — the DEGAMMA_LUT entry-count check and
# the _dpu_crtc_check_cp_blocks() call. Everything else is the author's work unchanged.
#
# SM8650 VERIFIED 2026-09-13 on Pocket FIT, which was the point of taking it. The author verified
# engine v2 on SM8550 (Odin 2 Portal) and v3 on SM8750 (Odin 3); SM8650 is DPU 10.0, also engine
# v3, and its catalog entry was carried but never run. It works: `engine v30000 sb_off 0x800`,
# opmode 0x1, intr4_status 0x0, ctl0 done 1, both topology DSPPs carrying IGC + gamut, and the two
# new CRTC properties sized exactly right (DEGAMMA_LUT 2048 B = 256x4xu16, DPU_3D_LUT 39312 B =
# 17^3x4xu16). ZERO lutdma/reg_dma/CTL_FLUSH/underrun/SMMU-fault lines across 16 enable/disable
# transitions, every one of which is a modeset.
# POCKET S2 PASSED 2026-09-14, closing the gate bc4f7b0's message left open, and it is the board
# that mattered most here: --rotated-output-max-height clamps its 1440x2560 panel to a 1080 render
# that the DPU upscales, so a pre-rotation height of 1080 sits 8 lines under the inline rotator's
# 1088 cap and anything that grew a layer would re-open the black-UI failure. It did not. In ALL
# FOUR states (LUTs on/off x night mode, plus colour temperature and the restore) the board held
# TWO live content planes BOTH at rotation=8 — inline rotation and the DPU upscale intact — with
# ZERO "invalid height for inline rot" rejections in dmesg and zero in the session log. Engine v3,
# opmode 0x1, intr4_status 0x0, zero display/LUTDMA errors after t=10s. Its one boot-time *ERROR*
# (`dsi_get_phy: phy driver is not ready`, t=1.0s on ae96000.dsi) is ordinary deferred probe on the
# secondary DSI and predates this work.
# POCKET ACE (qcs8550, DPU 9.0) ALSO PASSED, same day — engine v2, i.e. the AUTHOR's own verified
# configuration, so it re-gates their leg against our re-roll rather than testing new silicon:
# `engine v20000 sb_off 0x400` exactly as sm8550_reg_dma declares, opmode 0x1, intr4_status 0x0,
# ctl0 done 1 with intr_status 0x200000 = BIT(21) = v2's CTL_0 done mask on every ctl row, because
# v2 shares ONE status register where v3 has a per-CTL window. One DSPP for the single-mixer
# 1080x1620 panel vs two on the FIT, identical buffer indices. Zero display/LUTDMA errors after
# boot. Its 10 arm-smmu faults inside the first second are the PRE-EXISTING splash handoff noted
# above, unchanged in count — do not read them as LUTDMA fallout.
# No Kconfig symbol is added: the engine builds unconditionally into the msm driver (Makefile only),
# so the 426-symbol sweep below is unaffected. A failed LUTDMA wait/setup skips that DSPP while the
# commit still reports success, which the author bounds with a retry budget on the CRTC — that is
# part of 0530, not an optional follow-up, and it is the failure mode to watch for on new silicon.
#
# ============ THE GATE ITSELF: how to run it, and the ways it lies (any pin) ============
#
# The numbers each release produced are NOT kept here; the CURRENT pin's are in its section above.
# What follows is the method and the traps, which do not change between releases.
#
#   - CONFIG SWEEP. Every CONFIG_* named in kernel.config + trim-platforms.config must exist in the
#     PATCHED tree. Recipe: strip `CONFIG_x=` and `# CONFIG_x is not set` to bare names, diff
#     against every `config`/`menuconfig` name in the tree's Kconfig files. It must run against the
#     PATCHED tree, not a pristine one — some symbols are defined by our own patches, e.g.
#     AYANEO_SERIAL_MCU comes from 1005-misc-add-ayaneo-serial-mcu.patch. The count moves for OUR
#     reasons far more often than upstream's: every historical move so far was a fragment edit of
#     ours, not a symbol upstream deleted.
#     The one upstream casualty to date stays deleted and its note is still why — see
#     kernel.config's header:
#       READ_ONLY_THP_FOR_FS -> removed upstream in 7.2; file THP is unconditional now.
#     SCHED_CACHE (added 2026-08-22) still resolves the same way (init/Kconfig, `default y`,
#     `depends on SMP`) — the pin records an inherited default rather than changing one.
#   - PATCH APPLY. Every kernel/patches/*.patch must apply CUMULATIVELY, in lexical order, to ONE
#     tree, with zero rejects — see HOW TO RE-RUN below for why any other order lies.
#   - FUZZ, and why the total is the WRONG instrument. kernel/build.sh passes no -F0, so patch's
#     default fuzz=2 is accepted silently; when a hunk that matters reaches 2, patch starts
#     applying it in the WRONG PLACE and the build still succeeds. But the raw count has been
#     unmoved across several releases in a row, and a hunk sliding 1 -> 2 while another slid 2 -> 1
#     would leave it unmoved too. So diff the PER-HUNK PROFILE — patch name + hunk number + fuzz
#     level, sorted — between a pristine OLD tree and a pristine NEW one, both drilled today. Any
#     line that moves is a hunk to read.
#   - "0 REJECTS" DOES NOT PROVE A PATCH LANDED. For every patch you changed, and for every patch
#     whose file upstream touched, assert its CODE is present in the patched tree by grepping for
#     an identifier it introduces. A reversed-and-skipped patch also reports no reject.
#   - RE-CHECK EVERY CARRIED-FROM-A-LIST PATCH AGAINST ITS AUTHOR, NOT ITS THREAD. For each patch
#     we carry that is not upstream, look up the AUTHOR's other postings —
#     `patchwork.kernel.org/api/1.2/patches/?submitter=<id>&per_page=100`, the submitter id coming
#     from `/api/1.2/patches/?msgid=<our msgid>`. A patch its author has SUPERSEDED looks exactly
#     like one awaiting review: one message in the thread, no replies, state `new`, and the bug
#     still unfixed in every tree you can grep. The supersession lives in a DIFFERENT thread, often
#     a different subsystem, sometimes days later. 0508 was carried for two bumps in that state.
#     Two more traps in the same instrument: patchwork's `handled-elsewhere` means "another
#     project's row owns this", NOT "applied"; and lore's SEARCH is behind Anubis (HTTP 403) while
#     `b4 mbox <msgid>` and `/all/<msgid>/t.mbox.gz` are not gated — use b4 to read a thread, and
#     the patchwork API to enumerate an author.
#   - THE UPSTREAM-DELTA INTERSECTION, AND RUN IT FIRST. Take the `+++ b/` paths out of the
#     release's incremental patch (https://cdn.kernel.org/pub/linux/kernel/v7.x/incr/patch-A-B.xz)
#     and intersect with the `+++ b/` paths in kernel/patches/*.patch. It is far cheaper than
#     diffing two patched trees and it is the ONLY gate that reaches upstream's own changes: at
#     7.2.5 the fuzz profile could not see the DSI blocker, because upstream changed a function
#     none of our patches contexts on, and the config sweep could not, because no symbol moved.
#     A NON-EMPTY RESULT IS A READING ASSIGNMENT, NOT A NOTE. Read every file it names, and answer
#     BOTH questions it raises: did upstream break one of ours, and did one of ours land upstream
#     (in which case it is a deletion candidate, not a patch to carry forward).
#   - WHAT THE INTERSECTION CANNOT SEE, and therefore what the HW gate is actually for: a release
#     also changes drivers we depend on but carry no patch against, so nothing offline flags them.
#     Scan the ChangeLog for the subsystems these boards live on — i2c/qcom-geni (every touchscreen
#     and DDIC here sits behind geni, so a timing change is the likeliest source of a "touch works
#     but flakes"), soc/qcom/geni-se, media/qcom/iris (our =m decoder on all three SoCs; exercise
#     it with ffmpeg v4l2m2m and NOT Steam, see the iris/venus node interlock in build.sh),
#     remoteproc/qcom_q6v5_pas, usb typec/tcpm qcom-pmic — and say in the pin that they were looked
#     at, so the next reader knows they were not missed.
#   - `patch unexpectedly ends in middle of line` x4 is NOT a reject: patches 0004 (IFPC) and 0203
#     (S16_LE) have no trailing newline. Both hunks succeed. Pre-existing, unrelated to any bump.
#   - SYMBOL EXISTENCE IS NOT SYMBOL VALUE. The sweep above only proves a symbol is still defined
#     somewhere. The fragments must then be MERGED FOR REAL inside build/Dockerfile
#     (`merge_config.sh -m defconfig kernel/*.config` + `make olddefconfig`, trim-platforms.config
#     included) and every requested symbol asserted to have KEPT its requested value —
#     olddefconfig silently DROPS a =y symbol whose dependencies are unmet.
#     DEBUG_INFO_BTF/SCHED_CLASS_EXT resolve correctly only because the container ships `dwarves`
#     (pahole v1.31).
#   - READ `n` AND `undef` AS DIFFERENT FACTS, not as pass/fail. A trim-platforms.config directive
#     can come back `undef` (absent from .config entirely) rather than `n`, when some symbol the
#     same file already cuts gates it — that line then cuts nothing and should be deleted. `undef`
#     still PASSES, so an assertion demanding the literal `# ... is not set` line reports false
#     failures. build.sh counts the two apart and prints the redundant list; a REDUNDANT line in
#     the build log is a line to remove, not to ignore. Floor is 40 directives.
#   - FINALLY, BUILD IT. `make kernel` is the gate the offline drill cannot give you. Record the
#     warning count and WHICH FILES they come from: warnings in our own out-of-tree drivers cannot
#     be bump fallout, warnings in files the release touched can be.
#
# HOW TO RE-RUN THIS VALIDATION, and the mistake that makes it lie: patches must be applied
# cumulatively to ONE tree, exactly as kernel/build.sh does. Dry-running each patch independently
# against a pristine tree reports ~8 false failures, because several patches depend on files an
# earlier patch CREATES (1002 patches the qcom-hv-haptics.c that 1000 adds; 1003 patches the
# rsinput.c that 0031 adds) and the drm/panel patches all extend one Makefile, each one's
# context containing the line its predecessor added. Zero of those are real.
#
# Corrections applied during the 7.1.5 validation, still current and still load-bearing in
# kernel.config — do not "restore" the left-hand names:
#   CRYPTO_MICHAEL_MIC  -> DROPPED (upstream removed it from crypto/Kconfig; mac80211 carries its
#                          own michael_mic, so TKIP is unaffected). Was already a dead symbol in
#                          7.1.3 and silently ignored by kconfig.
#   MFD_QCOM_SPMI_PMIC  -> MFD_SPMI_PMIC
#   PHY_QCOM_SNPS_EUSB2 -> PHY_SNPS_EUSB2 (+ PHY_QCOM_EUSB2_REPEATER)
#   SM_DISPCC_8650      -> SM_DISPCC_8550 (SM8650 reuses the 8550 display clock controller;
#                          no separate _8650 dispcc upstream — symbols jump 8550 -> 8750)
#
# --- What 7.2 took over from us, and what that cost ---
# Two of our patches went upstream in 7.2 and were DELETED here rather than carried:
#   0101 (sm8550 DDR/LLCC/L3 CPU bandwidth scaling) — landed verbatim; all 11 hunks read as
#         already-applied against 7.2's sm8550.dtsi. Pure deletion, no other change.
#   0065 (sgm3804 panel-power regulator) — landed as a REWRITE, not our version, and with an
#         INCOMPATIBLE DT binding. Upstream's driver registers TWO rails from `pos`/`neg` child
#         nodes each carrying `enable-gpios`; ours was one rail with both GPIOs in `reset-gpios`
#         on the parent, hardcoding 5.2V (reg 0x0c) over I2C while telling the framework 5.0V.
#         Keeping both was not an option: same compatible, same file path, duplicate Kconfig
#         symbol. So the binding was migrated across all six board nodes and the panels that
#         consume it — see the HW gate below, this is the risky half of the bump.
#
# HW GATE, PARTLY RUN (3 of 7) as of 2026-09-09. The sgm3804 migration changes how panel power is
# brought up on Pocket ACE / DMG / DS / S1K / S2K, Pocket S2 and konkr-pf. Three of those seven —
# Pocket ACE, Pocket S2 and konkr-pf (Pocket FIT) — came up clean on the 2026-09-09 card, which is
# the failure this gate was written to catch (a lit backlight over a black panel, AVEE dead). DMG,
# DS, S1K and S2K are STILL UNTESTED, and they are the ones to try next:
#   - `avdd-supply` now points at the positive rail only; the negative rail is a SECOND regulator
#     that nothing enabled before, so every panel driver that used to get both rails from one
#     `avdd` now takes an `avee` supply too (patches 0051/0052/0054/0066/0067/0068). A board left
#     un-migrated would come up with AVEE dead — a lit backlight over a black panel.
#   - the rails are pinned to 5200000 uV, NOT the 5000000 the old DTS asked for. 5.2V is what the
#     hardware was actually running: our driver ignored the DT constraint and wrote selector 0x0c,
#     which is 4.0V + 12*100mV on upstream's linear range. Asking for 5.0V here would be a real,
#     silent voltage change dressed up as a no-op.
#   - upstream's driver declares enable_time = 40ms per rail; ours had none. regulator_bulk_enable
#     is async so both rails come up in parallel, but panel prepare is now ~40ms slower.
#
# The migration above was CROSS-CHECKED against the not-yet-merged upstream Pocket S2 display
# series (Neil Armstrong, Linaro), which is the reference for how these nodes are meant to look:
#   [v5] arm64: dts: qcom: sm8650-ayaneo-pocket-s2: add display nodes -- 2026-07-28, state `new`
#   https://patchwork.kernel.org/project/linux-arm-msm/patch/20260728-topic-sm8650-ayaneo-pocket-s2-display-dt-v5-1-892ab9bcecfe@linaro.org/
# (lore.kernel.org is behind an Anubis proof-of-work wall and cannot be fetched; patchwork serves
# the same mail and is not gated. Use it. SHARPENED 2026-09-12 while pulling the v3 SMMU series:
# under /api/1.2/ it is the JSON that works, NOT the mail endpoints — /api/1.2/patches/<id>/mbox/
# and .../raw/ both 404. Go
#   /api/1.2/patches/?msgid=<msgid, no angle brackets>   -> patch id(s) + the series id
#   /api/1.2/series/<sid>/                               -> every patch in the series, in order
#   /api/1.2/patches/<id>/                               -> `content` (the mail body) + `diff`
#   /api/1.2/covers/<id>/                                -> the cover letter, `content` = changelog
# A cover letter is NOT indexed as a patch, so a 0/N msgid returns an empty list — query 1/N
# instead, or take the series id off any patch in it. The `diff` field is byte-exact: the five
# diffs in 0515 were written straight out of it and apply at fuzz 0.
#
# CORRECTED 2026-09-13: `b4` IS THE RIGHT TOOL AND IT WORKS HERE. The Anubis wall is on lore's
# HTML only; the mbox endpoint it actually uses (/all/<msgid>/t.mbox.gz) is NOT gated, so
#   b4 am -o <dir> <msgid-without-angle-brackets>
# fetches a whole series in one call, applies the review trailers posted on the list, and checks
# attestation. That is one command against the patchwork recipe above, which needs three API round
# trips and a 0/N special case. Prefer b4; keep the patchwork route as the fallback for when only
# a patchwork id is known. Verified by re-fetching the v3 SMMU series: the per-patch diffs b4
# produces are byte-identical to the ones already in 0515 (only the blank line between
# concatenated patches differs, which we strip).
# The /project/<p>/patch/<msgid>/ web URLs above stay valid for humans.)
# It independently confirms 5200000 uV on both rails and the same tlmm 59=pos / 58=neg order we
# already had, and it carries three properties this tree was missing, now added to all six nodes:
#   vin-supply = <&vph_pwr>       the chip's input rail (label reachable on every board with an
#                                 sgm3804: qcs8550-ayaneo-pocket-common.dtsi + sm8650-ayaneo-common.dtsi)
#   regulator-active-discharge = <1>   restores the discharge bits our old driver wrote to reg 0x03
#                                 unconditionally; upstream only writes them when DT asks
#   regulator-boot-on             the rail can already be up at handoff (bootloader splash)
#
# WHERE WE STILL DIVERGE, deliberately: upstream names the panel's rails `vsp-supply`/`vsn-supply`
# and drives the S2 panel with a generic `renesas,r63419` driver (compatible "ayaneo,wt0630-2k",
# "renesas,r63419"). Neither that driver nor that series is in 7.2, so our six panel drivers stay
# and keep their own `avdd`/`avee` names. If we ever drop them for the upstream r63419 driver, the
# DTS supply names have to become vsp/vsn at the same time — they are a driver-side contract.
